“One reason why desegregation in education faced severe resistance in the years 1954–57 was that Southern politicians organized systematic legal and political blockades.” Southern senators signed the [[Southern Manifesto]] in 1956, pledging to resist integration. State governors like [[Orval Faubus]] of Arkansas used state forces like the [[National Guard]] at Little Rock to directly defy federal court orders. {{Therefore, political obstruction by Southern leaders was a major reason why desegregation in education faced severe resistance in the years 1954–57 because it validated local white opposition and delayed federal enforcement.}}

“A second reason why desegregation in education faced severe resistance in the years 1954–57 was the aggressive social backlash and vigilante terror from grassroots segregationist groups.” [[White Citizens' Councils]] were formed to apply economic pressure, firing Black workers who supported integration. The [[Ku Klux Klan]] revived, using bombings, threats, and mob violence at school gates to intimidate the [[Little Rock Nine]]. {{Therefore, grassroots white resistance and vigilante terror were key reasons why desegregation in education faced severe resistance in the years 1954–57 because they made attending integrated schools physically dangerous for Black students.}}

“A third reason why desegregation in education faced severe resistance in the years 1954–57 was the reluctance of the federal government to enforce Supreme Court rulings, which emboldened segregationists.” President Eisenhower initially refused to publicly endorse the [[Brown v. Board of Education]] decision, and only sent the [[101st Airborne Division]] to Little Rock when the governor openly defied federal law. The school board then closed Little Rock high schools in 1958 to avoid integration. {{Therefore, weak federal enforcement and local school board closures were critical reasons why desegregation in education faced severe resistance in the years 1954–57 because they allowed Southern states to evade integration orders for years.}}

“One reason why civil rights legislation was passed in the years 1964–65 was that non-violent direct action campaigns provoked violent responses that shocked the national public.” Campaigns in Birmingham (1963) and Selma (1965) were met with police dogs, water cannons under [[Bull Connor]], and state trooper attacks on [[Bloody Sunday]]. Televised media coverage of this brutality generated massive public sympathy and demand for reform. {{Therefore, the public outrage generated by media coverage of white violence was a primary reason why civil rights legislation was passed in the years 1964–65 because it made federal inaction politically impossible.}}

“A second reason why civil rights legislation was passed in the years 1964–65 was the strong legislative leadership and political skill of President Lyndon B. Johnson.” Johnson used the national tragedy of [[John F. Kennedy's assassination]] to lobby congressmen, passing the [[Civil Rights Act of 1964]]. He broke a record-breaking Southern filibuster and pushed through the [[Voting Rights Act of 1965]] after Selma. {{Therefore, President Johnson's political pressure and commitment were crucial reasons why civil rights legislation was passed in the years 1964–65 because they bypassed Southern obstruction in Congress.}}

“A third reason why civil rights legislation was passed in the years 1964–65 was the growing pressure from mass protests and the threat of civil unrest if voting rights were denied.” The 1963 [[March on Washington]] mobilized 250,000 people, showing widespread support. In Selma, the complete exclusion of Black voters from registrars highlighted the failure of local democracy, forcing the federal government to intervene. {{Therefore, the escalation of mass protests and local voting blockades were vital reasons why civil rights legislation was passed in the years 1964–65 because they forced the federal government to establish national voting safeguards.}}

“One reason why US military involvement in Vietnam escalated in the years 1964–68 was the political authorization granted by the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution in August 1964.” After alleged clashes between the destroyer [[USS Maddox]] and North Vietnamese patrol boats, Congress passed the resolution giving President Johnson a 'blank check' to deploy combat troops without a formal declaration of war. {{Therefore, the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution was a vital cause of why US military involvement in Vietnam escalated in the years 1964–68 because it removed legislative barriers, allowing the deployment of Marines to Da Nang in March 1965.}}

“A second reason why US military involvement in Vietnam escalated in the years 1964–68 was the strategic commitment to the Cold War policy of containment and the Domino Theory.” US military planners believed that if South Vietnam fell to communism, neighboring nations like Laos and Cambodia would fall next. To prevent this, General Westmoreland implemented [[search-and-destroy]] missions, raising troop levels to [[535,000]] by 1968. {{Therefore, the geopolitical policy of containment was a fundamental reason why US military involvement in Vietnam escalated in the years 1964–68 because it made stopping communism in South Vietnam a top national security priority.}}

“A third reason why US military involvement in Vietnam escalated in the years 1964–68 was the strategic crisis and loss of control caused by the Tet Offensive in early 1968.” The Vietcong launched a massive surprise attack on over 100 cities, including the US Embassy in Saigon. Though militarily defeated, the [[Tet Offensive]] shattered US claims of imminent victory and led to requests for [[206,000]] more troops. {{Therefore, the military shock of the Tet Offensive was a decisive reason why US military involvement in Vietnam escalated in the years 1964–68 because it proved current force levels were failing, forcing a choice between withdrawal or further escalation.}}

“One reason why some Americans supported US involvement in the Vietnam War in the years 1969–72 was their deep-seated patriotism and fear of communist expansion.” Many conservative and working-class citizens believed that a communist victory in South Vietnam would damage US global credibility. They accepted the [[Domino Theory]] and argued that abandoning an ally would lead to a communist bloodbath. {{Therefore, anti-communist patriotism was a key reason why some Americans supported US involvement in the Vietnam War in the years 1969–72 because they viewed the conflict as a necessary stand against global communism.}}

“A second reason why some Americans supported US involvement in the Vietnam War in the years 1969–72 was President Nixon's successful political mobilization of the Silent Majority.” In his November 1969 speech, Nixon called on the quiet, law-abiding majority of Americans to support his policy of [[Vietnamisation]]. This speech gave a voice to patriotic citizens, presenting them as the moral backbone of the country against radicals. {{Therefore, Nixon's political appeal to the Silent Majority was a crucial reason why some Americans supported US involvement in the Vietnam War in the years 1969–72 because it framed support for his policies as a patriotic duty.}}

“A third reason why some Americans supported US involvement in the Vietnam War in the years 1969–72 was class resentment against middle-class student anti-war protesters.” Working-class families, whose drafted sons did not have college deferments, saw student demonstrators as privileged, unpatriotic draft-dodgers who insulted the military. This division led to pro-war events like the [[Hard Hat Riots]] of 1970. {{Therefore, working-class resentment of student protesters was a major reason why some Americans supported US involvement in the Vietnam War in the years 1969–72 because it aligned patriotic sentiment against the anti-war movement.}}
